# Migration files are identity-critical, not ordinary text.
#
# server/db/migrate.js stores a sha256 of each migration file's bytes in
# schema_migrations, and the production boot gate compares that number on every
# start: a recorded checksum that differs from the file on disk is reported as
# MIGRATION_DRIFT and the server refuses to serve traffic. That is the correct
# fail-closed behaviour, but it means a purely cosmetic change - Git applying a
# text conversion, an editor's line endings, another operator's core.autocrlf
# setting, or a `git add --renormalize` - can turn into a refused boot in
# production.
#
# Marking these files non-text makes the repository bytes and the working bytes
# agree on every machine, so a checksum only changes when the SQL really does.
server/db/migrations/*.sql -text
